From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: hermes <hermes@local>
Date: Sun, 22 Nov 2026 12:47:17 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] host: the vectored sends and receives reach the wire

The NPLN client's gRPC speaks in MsgHdr: one call carries the vectors. The host answered that call with a
flat -1, straight out of the offline cut, so the client's first frame — on Jamboree the 24-byte HTTP/2
preface — never reached the socket: the title connected, sent nothing, and then waited for a reply that
could not come, with one grpc thread spinning a core for the rest of the session. The server is not the
quiet one: a plaintext preface to nex.nextendo.network gets a SETTINGS frame back at once (measured), so
this was the whole distance between "connected" and "talking".

Both directions: the iovecs are summed for SendMsg (a short write leaves the rest, as send(2) says), the
first iovec is filled for RecvMsg and the peer name and its length go back when the title asked for them.
The layout is asserted in the unit, because an offset guessed wrong would read a length out of the game's
memory and move nothing at all — the same silence, one layer down.

Upstream-Status: fork-only
Reason: The NPLN client's gRPC speaks in MsgHdr: one call carries the vectors. The host answered that call with a
  flat -1, straight out of the offline cut, so the client's first frame (on Jamboree the 24-byte HTTP/2 preface) never
  reached the socket: the title connected, sent nothing, and then waited for a reply that could not come, with one grpc
  thread spinning a core for the rest of the session. The server is not the quiet one: a plaintext preface to
  nex.nextendo.network gets a SETTINGS frame back at once (measured), so this was the whole distance between
  "connected" and "talking". Both directions are served: the iovecs are summed for SendMsg (a short write leaves the
  rest, as send(2) says), the first iovec is filled for RecvMsg and the peer name and its length go back when the
